#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345757 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ae256269-0bec-5d57-ac4a-eb0b07926f6f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Critical Points and Then Classifying Them Using the Second Derivative Test"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6a698bda-6e9a-5e86-93ce-5485a0a270cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345759} {:answer/id #uuid "dfd79b53-cd56-5dfc-9979-b27d3d102d76" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345760} {:answer/id #uuid "dc4894a8-96a8-5034-a841-1ceacc27922c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345761} {:answer/id #uuid "e78b2263-c151-5f34-8c9f-69ee87eef710" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345762} {:answer/id #uuid "6718f0ba-1315-5099-8b1f-9dda88175b42" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345763}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dfd79b53-cd56-5dfc-9979-b27d3d102d76" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345760} :answer-field/id #uuid "9aa3728f-5676-5271-a84b-087affe49287" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345758}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "58e760c4-f329-5e64-bec0-d19376ff7221" :question/math-academy-id "q-22011" :question/problem "For the function $f(x)=12{x}^{3}-\\frac{{x}^{5}}{5},$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $x=-6$ is a critical point of $f(x)$\n- ${f}^{″}(-6)<0$\n- $f(x)$ has a relative minimum at $x=-6$" :question/worked-solution "We need to identify whether $x=-6$ is a critical point. If it is, we then need to classify it using the second derivative test.\n\nFirst, we need to identify the critical points. Differentiating $f(x)=12{x}^{3}-\\frac{{x}^{5}}{5},$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=36{x}^{2}-{x}^{4},\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}36{x}^{2}-{x}^{4} & =0 \\\\ -{x}^{2}({x}^{2}-36) & =0 \\\\ -{x}^{2}(x+6)(x-6) & =0 \\\\ x & =-6,0,6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have critical points at $x=-6,0,6.$ We're interested in the critical point $x=-6.$\n\nNext, we need to determine the sign of ${f}^{″}(x)$ at this critical point. Computing ${f}^{″}(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=72x-4{x}^{3},\n$$\n\nand evaluating ${f}^{″}(x)$ at $x=-6$, we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(-6)=432>0.\n$$\n\nThe second derivative is positive at $x=-6,$ so this critical point is a relative *minimum*. \n\nTherefore, comparing our results with the given statements, we conclude that:\n\n- $x=-6$ is a critical point of $f(x)\\,✓$\n\n- ${f}^{″}(-6)<0\\,\\times $\n\n- $f(x)$ has a relative *minimum* at $x=-6\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}]}
